C library for apps only (engines/lite/mobile, libkitten_tts08; not in any wheel):

- CEPhonemizer: engine/src/frontend/cephonemizer/ (phonemizer.cpp,
  phonemizer.h, rule_parser.h, ipa_table.h), KittenML's own C++ reader of
  espeak-ng's English pronunciation rules, copied from
  https://github.com/KittenML/KittenTTS-react-native (vendor/cephonemizer,
  commit c90442e; the same files as KittenTTS-swift Sources/CEPhonemizer).
  Copyright 2024 KittenML, Apache License 2.0; it contains no espeak-ng
  source files (its ASCII-to-IPA table follows espeak-ng's phoneme symbol
  mapping). Changed here (each change is marked "KittenML kitten-inference
  change" in the source): debug switches read only in debug builds; en-us
  "o@" written as in espeak-ng 1.52.0; en-us phrase variants ("?3 (...)") in
  en_list honoured; no -s stripping of two-letter stems; lower-case
  abbreviations spelt out; no exception on digit strings of more than 18
  digits; a read-only accessor for clause-final stress flags.
- engine/src/frontend/tiny_en (the English text front end of Kitten TTS 2
  mini, nano and pico) is a C++ port of misaki 0.9.4's English G2P
  (https://github.com/hexgrad/misaki, Apache License 2.0) as vendored in
  src/kitten_inference/tts2_tiny/frontend/misaki/en.py, and of that folder's
  english.py, tts_norm.py and bart_numpy.py (kittenml, Apache-2.0). Its
  tokenizer is a C++ port of spaCy's tokenizer algorithm and English
  punctuation rules (https://github.com/explosion/spaCy, MIT License,
  Copyright (C) 2016-2024 ExplosionAI GmbH, 2016 spaCy GmbH, 2015 Matthew
  Honnibal; licence text in licenses/spaCy.txt); no spaCy model is included.
  Its number words (num_words.cpp) are original code written from the
  expected English wording. The data
  it reads at run time (misaki's lexicons, CMUdict in misaki's alphabet, the
  BART weights, spaCy's tokenizer exceptions) is not part of the library: the
  app supplies it (engines/lite/mobile/README.md, "Text to phonemes").
- engine/src/frontend/espeak_lang.*, tiny_lang*.cpp and engine/src/frontend/lang/
  {da,fi,fr,hi,ko,pt,sv,sw}.* (the text front ends of Kitten TTS 2 mini, nano and
  pico for Spanish, Italian, Portuguese, French and German, and for mini Danish,
  Hindi, Korean and Swedish; the Finnish, Swahili and Turkish code is in the tree but not enabled) are original KittenML code (Apache-2.0)
  that reads espeak-ng's data files; no espeak-ng code is included, and the
  per-language behaviour was found by running the reference as a black box. The ASCII-to-IPA table in espeak_lang.cpp comes from CEPhonemizer
  (vendor/cephonemizer/ipa_table.h, Copyright 2024 KittenML, Apache-2.0).
- engine/src/frontend/lang/ja*.cpp (the Japanese text front end of Kitten
  TTS 2 mini) are original KittenML code (Apache-2.0): a reader of the
  compiled UniDic/MeCab dictionary files written from their format (the
  layout MeCab defines; MeCab is Copyright (c) 2001-2008 Taku Kudo and
  2004-2008 Nippon Telegraph and Telephone Corporation, offered under the
  BSD-3-Clause licence among others; no MeCab code is included or linked),
  and a C++ version of the package's Japanese path. Its
  kana-to-IPA table and rules come from cutlet.py (cutlet, MIT License,
  Copyright (c) 2020 Paul O'Leary McCann, as adapted in misaki 0.9.4,
  Apache-2.0), its number words from num2kana.py (Convert-Numbers-to-Japanese,
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2018 David Wilson) and its full-width table from
  mojimoji 0.0.13 (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2013 Studio Ousia); the MIT terms are in
  src/kitten_inference/tts2_tiny/frontend/NOTICE. ja_unicode_tables.inc is
  generated from the Unicode Character Database (Unicode License v3,
  https://www.unicode.org/license.txt) by engine/tools/gen_ja_unicode.py.
- The UniDic 2.1.2 dictionary (sys.dic, unk.dic, matrix.bin, char.bin from
  unidic-lite 1.0.8; UniDic Consortium, BSD-3-Clause or GPL/LGPL) is NOT part
  of this repository or of any build: apps download it at run time
  (mobile/fetch_phonemizer_data.sh ... ja), as the Python package does.
- ja_words.txt (the Japanese word list read by the Japanese front end) is misaki 0.9.4's file, already in this repository at
  src/kitten_inference/tts2_tiny/frontend/misaki/data/ja_words.txt under that package's NOTICE (Apache-2.0); the word list's own
  upstream provenance was not checked separately.
- espeak-ng's data files (en_rules, en_list and the other languages' rules,
  word lists and phoneme definitions; GPL-3.0) are NOT part of this
  repository or of any build. Apps download them at run time
  (mobile/fetch_phonemizer_data.sh) and pass their paths to
  kt08_phonemizer_load or their folder to kt08_tiny_frontend_load.

Android wheel (in kitten_inference_tts08.libs/):

- libc++_shared-*.so: the LLVM C++ standard library (libc++) from the Android
  NDK. Apache License 2.0 with LLVM Exceptions: licenses/libc++.txt.

Not included
------------
- Model weights. On first use the package downloads them from Hugging Face
  (KittenML/meownn-models, revision 5e9bca0cc3a4e2a617f604b1333d5f34536c1eed).
  They are the Kitten TTS 0.8 models, converted to this engine's format,
  which KittenML publishes under the Apache License, Version 2.0
  (https://huggingface.co/KittenML/kitten-tts-nano-0.8, -micro-0.8 and
  -mini-0.8, and their int8 versions).
- espeak-ng's data files (dictsource/<lang>_rules, <lang>_list, phsource/...),
  GPL-3.0 (https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng). The C library's text front
  end reads them at run time from a folder the app fills with
  mobile/fetch_phonemizer_data.sh (pinned commit, SHA-256 checked); they are
  not part of this repository, the library or any wheel.
- Runtime dependencies, which pip installs separately under their own
  licenses. Among them, phonemizer and the espeak-ng library shipped by
  espeakng_loader are GPL-3.0.
